 ## Context
 
-Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
+Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest. Kiosks have no network route during normal operation; signed USB snapshots are the only registry update channel.
 
 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed. The certificates node uses only this interface and does not read registry storage.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The active snapshot is backed by a local embedded SQLite index that the kiosk builds from that snapshot's records. Lookups read only files on the kiosk; no service and no runtime network access are involved.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the SQLite index for the new snapshot and prepare it without exposing partially imported records. Activate the snapshot and its index together only after all validation and index preparation succeed. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot and its index active and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the active snapshot's SQLite index for the normalized serial number. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup index | A local embedded SQLite index, built on the kiosk when a snapshot activates | An on-site HTTPS directory with a centrally maintained index; a managed search service | Kiosks have no network route during normal operation and no runtime network access is permitted (see HLD tenets), so a lookup cannot depend on the lab network or an external service. A local index gives predictable lookups and needs no service to operate; each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives. |
